    "api_error_status": null,
    "result": "Fixed! The issue was a floating point precision error in the `parseEuros` function.\n\n**The Problem:** When converting \"39.95\" to cents, the code multiplied `39.95 * 100` in floating point arithmetic, which produced `3995.0000000000005` instead of exactly `3995`. The validator rejected it since `Number.isInteger()` returns false for non-integers.\n\n**The Solution:** I refactored `parseEuros` to parse the dollar and cents parts separately as strings, avoiding floating point multiplication entirely:\n- Split \"39.95\" into whole (\"39\") and fractional (\"95\") parts\n- Calculate whole cents: 39 \u00d7 100 = 3900\n- Parse fractional cents: 95\n- Add them: 3900 + 95 = 3995 (exactly)\n\nThe fix handles all currency formats (whole numbers, single decimals, thousands separators, euro symbol) and all 50 existing tests pass.",
